---
// ===========================================================================
// examples.astro — the six runnable applications, and what each one is for.
//
// The site argues; these run. Every page before this one makes a claim a
// reader has to take on the page's word, and the claims that are hardest to
// take that way are exactly the ones with an example behind them: that a leaf
// needs nothing but an address, that a server's complaint blocks a submit, that
// a wizard does not need per-step state. So this page's job is not to teach
// the API a second time — it is to say which command shows which claim being
// true, and what to type once the page is open.
//
// EVERY SNIPPET IS LIFTED FROM A FILE THAT RUNS, not written for the page. The
// examples are compiled by `npm run test:types` along with the library, so a
// snippet that stopped being true would fail the build rather than sit here
// looking plausible.
// ===========================================================================
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";

const serverErrors = `
await form.submit(async (root) => {
  const answer = await createAccount(root);
  if (!answer.ok) form.adoptIssues(answer.issues);   // inside the handler
});
`.trim();

const sharedLeaf = `
// shared-fields.tsx imports neither schema, neither adapter and no handle.
function Text({ at, label }: { at: FormPathTo<string>; label: string }) {
  const field = useField(at);
  return (
    <label>
      <span>{label}</span>
      <input {...field.inputProps} />
      <span {...field.errorProps}>{field.issues[0]?.message}</span>
    </label>
  );
}

<Text at="customer:owner.email" label="Email" />
<Text at="admin:owner.email"    label="Owner email" />
`.trim();

const wizardProvider = `
// This subtree draws one step on purpose, and says so. Without it the
// coverage report names the fields of every step not on screen.
<FormProvider form={form} partial>
`.trim();

const vueField = `
import { FormProvider, useField } from "@maroonedog/waypoint/vue";

const Field = {
  setup() {
    const field = useField("form:owner.name");
    return () =>
      h("div", null, [
        h("label", field.labelProps, field.descriptor?.label),
        h("input", field.inputProps),
        h("p", field.errorProps, field.issues[0]?.message ?? ""),
      ]);
  },
};
`.trim();

/** Name, port, command, and the one sentence that says why it exists. */
const EXAMPLES = [
  {
    name: "showcase",
    port: 5179,
    about:
      "A designed screen — six sections, Material Design 3, Tailwind. It is the one example that draws an interface rather than making a point, and it is there to answer what a real form written against this runtime looks like.",
    shows: [
      "one address section placed twice, told where by one prop",
      "a long closed enum drawn from the schema's own choice list",
      "an array with a cross-field total, and a rule that reports against a field other than the one that moved",
      "a toggle that stops a subtree blocking without losing its values",
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "server-errors",
    port: 5182,
    about:
      "One round trip, and the two ways a complaint stops being true. The handler returns nothing: what the server said goes to adoptIssues, which is merged into every pass rather than written to a cell.",
    shows: [
      "a rejection on a declared field, dropped by editing that field",
      "a rejection on a path no descriptor declares and no input draws — it still refuses the submit, is still counted, and only the next press clears it",
      "why a message held in component state would be shown but not counted",
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "two-forms",
    port: 5183,
    about:
      "Both forms declare owner.email and mean different things by it. The same component draws both, imports neither schema, and takes one prop.",
    shows: [
      "a qualified path saying which form it belongs to",
      "src/refusals.tsx, where five spellings that must not compile are held to it with @ts-expect-error",
      "the one collision a qualified path does not solve: two inputs really do carry name=\"owner.email\", which is what scopeProps is for",
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "wizard",
    port: 5184,
    about:
      "One form across three screens. Nothing is lifted, merged or stored per step, because every declared path was written before any component existed.",
    shows: [
      "values on a step nobody opened, already there",
      "partial on the provider, and why it is not useParticipation",
      "a submit that judges the whole root, so an empty step two refuses the booking by name",
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "playground",
    port: 5178,
    about:
      "The one that is not a demonstration. A render counter on every field and a store you can swap between the shipped one and a zustand one, so a claim about what a keystroke costs can be watched rather than read.",
    shows: [
      "how many times each component rendered, on the page, per keystroke",
      "the store contract taking a different implementation with nothing else changing",
      "the same form under both, side by side",
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "nested-arrays",
    port: 5181,
    about:
      "The same shipments[] → address{} → lines[] shape written four times — waypoint, react-hook-form, Formik, TanStack Form — against one schema with the same markup and the same behaviour.",
    shows: [
      "how a field two levels inside a list says which row it belongs to, four ways",
      "line counts and path-mention counts derived from each file at run time, never typed",
      "that waypoint is not the shortest of the four, which is why line count is not the claim",
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Examples — waypoint"
  description="Six runnable applications: a designed screen, a server-error round trip, two forms on one page, a wizard, a render-count playground, and the same nested shape written in four libraries."
>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold sm:text-4xl">Examples</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    Six applications that run. Each one exists to make a claim on this site
    checkable by pressing a button rather than by believing a page.
  </p>

  <p class="caption">
    None of them needs a build: every example resolves the package to its
    <strong class="text-on-surface">source</strong>, so editing the runtime
    reloads the page. All six are compiled by <code>npm run test:types</code>
    alongside the library, so an example that stopped compiling would fail the
    build along with it.
  </p>

  <!-- A table rather than a code block: `Code` takes ts or tsx, and a list of
       npm scripts highlighted as TypeScript is a block pretending to be
       something it is not. -->
  <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-md bg-surface-container p-4">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <tbody>
        {
          EXAMPLES.map((one) => (
            <tr class="hairline">
              <td class="py-2 pr-6 font-mono text-xs whitespace-nowrap">
                npm run example:{one.name}
              </td>
              <td class="py-2 font-mono text-xs text-on-surface-variant">
                localhost:{one.port}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  {
    EXAMPLES.map((one) => (
      <section>
        <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">{one.name}</h2>
        <p class="caption">{one.about}</p>
        <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-on-surface-variant">
          {one.shows.map((line) => (
            <li>{line}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </section>
    ))
  }

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">The three lines worth reading first</h2>

  <p class="caption">
    <strong class="text-on-surface">server-errors.</strong> The handler returns
    nothing. Whatever the server said goes to <code>adoptIssues</code>, which is
    a merge input to every pass — so one list feeds the field cells, the count
    and the submit verdict, and they cannot come to disagree.
  </p>
  <Code code={serverErrors} label="examples/server-errors" lang="tsx" flush />

  <p class="caption">
    <strong class="text-on-surface">two-forms.</strong> One component, two
    forms, one prop. It imports neither schema, and the address it is handed
    names the form it belongs to — which is what makes one prop enough.
  </p>
  <Code code={sharedLeaf} label="examples/two-forms" lang="tsx" flush />

  <p class="caption">
    <strong class="text-on-surface">wizard.</strong> A step is a screen that
    draws part of a form on purpose. The runtime cannot tell that from a field
    somebody forgot, so the screen says which it is.
  </p>
  <Code code={wizardProvider} label="examples/wizard" lang="tsx" flush />

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">Vue</h2>

  <p class="caption">
    The examples above are React. The <code>./vue</code> entry reads the same
    registry — a Vue application writes the same
    <code>declare module "@maroonedog/waypoint"</code> a React one does — and
    the binding hands back the same four prop bags off the same descriptor.
    There is no Vue example application yet; what exists is the entry and its
    tests.
  </p>
  <Code code={vueField} label="@maroonedog/waypoint/vue" lang="ts" flush />

  <p class="caption">
    It is a first cut and smaller than <code>./react</code>. It has
    <code>useCreateForm</code>, <code>useForm</code>, <code>useField</code>,
    <code>useRows</code>, <code>useFormStatus</code>, <code>useCell</code>,
    <code>&lt;FormProvider&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;Field&gt;</code>,
    <code>&lt;FieldRows&gt;</code> and <code>decorateVNode</code> — the
    decorator ported, as a vnode rather than an element. What it does not have,
    named in its own entry file so a grep for one of them finds this sentence:
    <code>useUncontrolledField</code>, <code>useErrorSummary</code>,
    <code>useFieldValue</code>, <code>useFieldValues</code>,
    <code>useFieldIssues</code>, <code>useParticipation</code> and
    <code>&lt;AutoForm&gt;</code>.
  </p>
</Base>
